# Weather theory: FAA-H-8083-28A Aviation Weather Handbook and PHAK (FAA-H-8083-25C) chapter 12. ACS area I.C.
ITEMS = [
    # ---------- Practice Test 1 ----------
    S(1, 1, "Thunderstorms", "What three conditions are needed for a thunderstorm to form?",
      ["Sufficient moisture, an unstable lapse rate and a lifting force",
       "High humidity, a strong temperature inversion and light surface winds",
       "A cold front, freezing temperatures aloft and high pressure"],
      "Thunderstorms need enough water vapor, unstable air and a lifting action (fronts, terrain, heating or converging winds) to start the air rising."),
    S(1, 2, "Thunderstorms", "During which stage of a thunderstorm's life cycle do strong updrafts and downdrafts exist at the same time and precipitation begin to reach the ground?",
      ["The mature stage", "The cumulus stage", "The dissipating stage"],
      "The cumulus stage is mostly updrafts, the mature stage begins when precipitation reaches the surface and has both updrafts and downdrafts, and the dissipating stage is mainly downdrafts."),
    S(1, 2, "Stability", "Which weather would you expect in a stable air mass?",
      ["Stratiform clouds, smooth air and poor visibility in haze",
       "Cumuliform clouds, turbulence and showery precipitation",
       "Towering clouds, gusty winds and good visibility in showers"],
      "Stable air resists vertical motion, so it brings layered clouds, steady precipitation, smooth air and often restricted visibility. Unstable air brings cumuliform clouds, showers, turbulence and good visibility outside them."),
    S(1, 3, "Cloud bases", "The surface temperature is 22 degrees C and the dewpoint is 12 degrees C. About how high are the bases of convective clouds above the ground?",
      ["4,000 feet", "2,500 feet", "5,500 feet"],
      "Temperature and dewpoint converge at about 2.5 degrees C per 1,000 feet in rising unsaturated air. A 10 degree spread divided by 2.5 gives about 4,000 feet AGL."),
    S(1, 2, "Fog", "What type of fog forms when moist air moves over a colder surface, often along coasts, and can form with winds?",
      ["Advection fog", "Radiation fog", "Steam fog"],
      "Advection fog forms when moist air moves over colder ground or water and can persist with winds up to about 15 knots. Radiation fog forms on clear, calm nights over land."),
    S(1, 2, "Icing", "What is required for structural icing to form on an airplane?",
      ["Visible moisture and an airframe temperature at or below freezing",
       "High relative humidity and an air temperature below minus 20 degrees C",
       "Any cloud layer combined with a temperature inversion aloft"],
      "Structural ice needs visible moisture (cloud, rain or drizzle) and an aircraft surface at or below 0 degrees C. Freezing rain forms the fastest, most dangerous clear ice."),
    S(1, 3, "Wind shear", "Where can wind shear occur?",
      ["At any altitude, in any direction",
       "Only near thunderstorms at low altitude",
       "Only in the vicinity of a frontal inversion"],
      "Wind shear is a sudden change in wind speed or direction over a short distance and can occur at any altitude, horizontally or vertically. It is most dangerous close to the ground."),
    # ---------- Practice Test 2 ----------
    S(2, 1, "Standard atmosphere", "What are the standard sea level temperature and pressure in the International Standard Atmosphere?",
      ["15 degrees C and 29.92 inches of mercury",
       "15 degrees C and 30.92 inches of mercury",
       "59 degrees C and 29.92 inches of mercury"],
      "The ISA standard at sea level is 15 degrees C (59 degrees F) and 29.92 inches of mercury (1013.2 hectopascals), with temperature decreasing about 2 degrees C per 1,000 feet."),
    S(2, 2, "Fronts", "Which weather is typical of a fast-moving cold front?",
      ["A narrow band of cumuliform clouds with showers and gusty winds",
       "A wide band of stratus clouds with steady drizzle and fog",
       "Clearing skies ahead of the front with calm winds behind it"],
      "A fast-moving cold front lifts warm air sharply, producing a narrow band of cumulus or cumulonimbus with showers, thunderstorms and gusts, then clearing and cooler air behind it."),
    S(2, 2, "Frost", "How does frost on the wings affect an airplane during takeoff?",
      ["It disrupts smooth airflow, reducing lift and raising stall speed",
       "It adds weight but has no measurable effect on the airflow",
       "It smooths the wing surface, which slightly increases the lift produced"],
      "Even thin frost roughens the wing, disrupting airflow, decreasing lift, increasing drag and raising stall speed. It must be removed before flight."),
    S(2, 3, "Temperature inversion", "A pilot sees a layer of smoke and haze trapped near the ground on a calm morning. What does this suggest?",
      ["A temperature inversion with stable air near the surface",
       "Unstable air with strong vertical mixing near the surface",
       "An approaching cold front with gusty winds below the layer"],
      "In an inversion temperature rises with height, which makes the air below very stable and traps smoke, haze and pollutants, reducing visibility."),
    S(2, 2, "Mountain wave", "Standing lens-shaped clouds over and downwind of a mountain range indicate",
      ["mountain wave activity with possible severe turbulence",
       "stable air with smooth flying conditions at all levels",
       "an approaching warm front with steady light rain"],
      "Lenticular clouds form in the crests of mountain waves. They suggest strong winds over the ridge and possible severe turbulence, including in rotor clouds below them."),
    S(2, 2, "Local winds", "On a sunny afternoon at a coastal airport, the wind usually blows",
      ["from the water toward the land", "from the land toward the water", "parallel to the coastline only"],
      "Land heats faster than water in the day, so air rises over land and cooler air flows in from the water as a sea breeze. At night the flow reverses to a land breeze."),
    S(2, 3, "Microbursts", "Flying into a microburst on approach, what does an airplane usually meet first?",
      ["An increasing headwind and a rise in airspeed",
       "A sudden tailwind and a sharp loss of airspeed",
       "A strong downdraft with no change in airspeed"],
      "Entering a microburst, an airplane first meets the outflow as a headwind (airspeed and performance increase), then a strong downdraft, then a tailwind that sharply reduces airspeed near the ground."),
    # ---------- Drill only ----------
    S(0, 1, "Pressure", "In the Northern Hemisphere, how does the wind blow around a high pressure area?",
      ["Clockwise and outward", "Counterclockwise and inward", "Clockwise and inward"],
      "Air flows out of a high and, turned by the Coriolis force, circulates clockwise in the Northern Hemisphere. Around a low it flows counterclockwise and inward."),
    S(0, 2, "Dew and frost", "Frost forms on surfaces when the temperature of the collecting surface is",
      ["at or below the dewpoint, and the dewpoint is below freezing",
       "above the dewpoint, and the air temperature is below freezing",
       "above freezing, and the air is saturated with water vapor"],
      "Frost forms by deposition when the surface cools to or below the dewpoint and the dewpoint itself is below freezing, so water vapor turns directly to ice."),
    S(0, 2, "Fog", "Radiation fog is most likely to form",
      ["over low, flat land on clear, calm nights with high humidity",
       "over coastal areas when moist air moves inland with wind",
       "on mountain slopes when moist, stable air is pushed upward by the wind"],
      "Radiation (ground) fog forms when the ground cools on a clear night and chills the air in light or calm wind until it reaches the dewpoint. Moist air moved by wind gives advection or upslope fog."),
    S(0, 2, "Lapse rate", "Unsaturated air that rises cools at about",
      ["3 degrees C per 1,000 feet", "2 degrees C per 1,000 feet", "1.5 degrees C per 1,000 feet"],
      "The dry adiabatic lapse rate is about 3 degrees C (5.4 degrees F) per 1,000 feet. Saturated air cools more slowly, and the standard average lapse rate is about 2 degrees C."),
    S(0, 3, "Icing", "Ice pellets reported at the surface usually indicate",
      ["freezing rain at a higher altitude", "a thunderstorm in the vicinity", "a warm front has already passed the station"],
      "Ice pellets form when rain falls from warmer air aloft through a layer below freezing. Their presence means there is freezing rain and warmer air somewhere above."),
    S(0, 1, "Thunderstorms", "What is a narrow, non-frontal band of active thunderstorms, which often contains the most severe weather, called?",
      ["A squall line", "An occluded front", "A dry line"],
      "A squall line is a non-frontal line of thunderstorms that often holds the most intense weather. An occluded front is frontal, and a dry line is a moisture boundary along which storms may form."),
    S(0, 2, "Turbulence", "When can a pilot expect turbulence close to a thunderstorm?",
      ["Within 20 miles of a severe storm, even in clear air",
       "Only inside the cloud or under its precipitation",
       "Only on the downwind side of the storm, under its anvil cloud"],
      "Severe turbulence can extend well beyond a thunderstorm. The AIM advises avoiding severe storms by at least 20 nautical miles."),
    S(0, 2, "Air masses", "Moist, unstable air flowing up a mountain slope tends to produce",
      ["cumuliform clouds with showers and turbulence",
       "stratus clouds with drizzle and smooth air",
       "clear skies with no change in the weather"],
      "Lifting unstable moist air makes it rise freely, forming cumuliform clouds, showery precipitation and turbulence. Lifting stable air would give stratiform clouds."),
    S(0, 3, "Microbursts", "About how long does an individual microburst usually last from the time it first strikes the ground?",
      ["Seldom more than 15 minutes", "About 45 minutes to an hour", "Up to 2 hours"],
      "The AIM describes an individual microburst as seldom lasting longer than 15 minutes from first ground contact to dissipation, with its strongest winds about 5 minutes after it touches down."),
    S(0, 1, "Clouds", "Which cloud type indicates very unstable air and the most hazardous flying conditions?",
      ["Cumulonimbus", "Nimbostratus", "Altostratus"],
      "Cumulonimbus are thunderstorm clouds with turbulence, icing, hail, lightning and wind shear. Nimbostratus and altostratus are layered clouds in more stable air."),
]
